*Context: *Much thanks to everyone who voted for last week=E2=80=99s Around=
 the
Horn choices. Usually, I can trust my readers to pick the most technical
article, but I was pleasantly surprised your collective preference for the
human interest angle. Told to =E5=86=B7=E6=9D=89RECORD, sociology professor=
 Qiaoqiao=C2=B9
grapples with how her 211 university (~top 100 institution, not as good as
the elite 985 universities) is dealing with the rise of AI. Here=E2=80=99s =
the
lead-in to her reflections:

Qiaoqiao=E2=80=99s predicament is a microcosm of the current situation for
humanities and social science teachers in universities. What we want to
explore further is when this data has become the standard for measuring
everything, how much space can be reserved for values that cannot be
quantified=E2=80=94freedom of thought, humanistic concern, and critical thi=
nking?
In this crisis in the humanities, the real damage may not just be to a few
individuals or a few majors, but rather that universities are losing their
most precious function: cultivating independent thinkers.

*Key Takeaways: AI is warping the current teaching assessment system for
humanities and social science (**wenke**) professors at Qiaoqiao=E2=80=99s
university.*

   -

   Previously, a supervisor would conduct a classroom observation, but now:
   =E2=80=9C*There are cameras in the classrooms; every class is recorded a=
nd
   uploaded to the cloud. Supervisors can listen to the classes in real tim=
e
   online, without the teachers knowing. It was only after the semester end=
ed
   that I learned that over 20 supervisors had once audited my online class=
*
   .=E2=80=9D
   -

   Starting this semester, AI tools are tracking her student engagement
   rates: =E2=80=9C*It can recognize surveillance footage and count how man=
y
   students look up when you=E2=80=99re explaining a particular concept*.=
=E2=80=9D The
   lesson is disassembled into components that can be quantified: several
   minutes must be dedicated to a story-based introduction and several minu=
tes
   to explaining key concepts.

*Qiaoqiao and her colleagues complain that most supervisors have STEM
backgrounds, so they don=E2=80=99t understand the teaching approach of the
humanities and social sciences.*

   -

   For example, in a course about research methods in which she discussed
   how to conduct fieldwork in villages, Qiaoqiao once received the followi=
ng
   comment, =E2=80=9C*Too high and mighty, just talking about her research
   experience in class*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Qiaoqiao recalls another case: =E2=80=9C*I mentioned choosing a field si=
te in
   class and concluded with a reflection, =E2=80=98Sometimes where you choo=
se to do
   your fieldwork is actually an arrangement of fate.=E2=80=99 The supervis=
or seized
   on this and wrote in their comments that I was promoting fatalism with
   students and that there were problems with my ideology. I was so angry I
   laughed.=E2=80=9D*
   -

   From the article: =E2=80=9C*In her view, university education should be =
the
   transmission of =E2=80=98Technique and Tao=E2=80=99=E2=80=94the former b=
eing methods and skills,
   the latter the wisdom to understand society and care for oneself and
   others. However, these elements, which cannot be simplified into key
   knowledge points, have lost their place in the new evaluation system*.=
=E2=80=9D
   Qiaoqiao muses, =E2=80=9C*Perhaps it=E2=80=99s because universities have=
 become
   increasingly strict in controlling the content and ideas of teaching, so
   they don=E2=80=99t want teachers to have too much room for personal expr=
ession*.=E2=80=9D

*Alongside the rise of AI, changes in the overall economic landscape have
challenged many university humanities and social science programs.*

   -

   Background context from the article: =E2=80=9C*Since this summer, at lea=
st five
   universities have suspended undergraduate admissions for sociology
   programs, and many more arts and humanities programs face the risk of
   closure*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   Qiaoqiao=E2=80=99s sociology program is still safe for now, but one of h=
er
   primary tasks is =E2=80=9Cstrictly controlling the rate of students chan=
ging
   majors.=E2=80=9D She says, =E2=80=9C*The transfer rate of a class will a=
ffect the
   discipline evaluation and even determine whether the program can continu=
e
   to exist. If our major is canceled, our jobs might be in jeopardy, or we
   might be reassigned to other colleges like Marxism or Public
   Administration, where survival would be even more difficult*.=E2=80=9D

*Even as she realizes that the expansion of humanities enrollment may need
to adjust based on market conditions, Qiaoqiao wants her students to have
the space to read good books, find interesting questions, and seek meaning
=E2=80=94 to gain a =E2=80=9Cusefulness of uselessness=E2=80=9D mindset.*

   -

   She relays, =E2=80=9C*Sometimes I feel that universities don=E2=80=99t n=
eed to admit so
   many people to the humanities, nor do they need so many full-time
   researchers. There=E2=80=99s no need for everyone to crowd into one trac=
k,
   producing things that are meaningless to the world. The humanities need =
a
   freer space to cultivate true masters. But the current environment makes=
 me
   feel that university humanities are becoming increasingly vulgar and
   narrow-minded*.=E2=80=9D
   -

   She concludes, =E2=80=9C*More importantly, when society is so technology=
-driven,
   and everyone adheres to a single evaluation system, there=E2=80=99s a ne=
ed for some
   forces from the humanities to step forward and tell you that people
   shouldn=E2=80=99t live like this=E2=80=A6I recall a few days ago, when I=
 was talking to a
   STEM student about future retirement issues, he said, =E2=80=98Why are y=
ou so
   worried? Once we have robots, everything will be fine.=E2=80=99 In the e=
yes of a
   humanities student, this answer naturally needs to be questioned. Lately=
,
   for some reason, (Max) Weber=E2=80=99s quote keeps echoing in my mind: =
=E2=80=9Cspecialists
   without spirit, sensualists without heart; this nullity imagines that it
   has attained a level of civilization never before achieved.=E2=80=9D And=
 the value
   of the humanities today lies precisely in helping us find our own soul a=
nd
   heart.*

