pan></p><blockquote style=3D"border-left:4px solid #4dd0e1;margin:20px 0;pa=
dding:0"><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);margin-left:20px=
;line-height:26px;font-size:16px">Qiaoqiao=E2=80=99s predicament is a micro=
cosm of the current situation for humanities and social science teachers in=
 universities. What we want to explore further is when this data has become=
 the standard for measuring everything, how much space can be reserved for =
values that cannot be quantified=E2=80=94freedom of thought, humanistic con=
cern, and critical thinking? In this crisis in the humanities, the real dam=
age may not just be to a few individuals or a few majors, but rather that u=
niversities are losing their most precious function: cultivating independen=
t thinkers.</p></blockquote><p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,5=
5);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>Key Takeaways: AI is warping th=
e current teaching assessment system for humanities and social science (</s=
trong><em><strong>wenke</strong></em><strong>) professors at Qiaoqiao=E2=80=
=99s university.</strong></p><ul style=3D"margin-top:0;padding:0"><li style=
=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;m=
argin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin=
:0"><span>Previously, a supervisor would conduct a classroom observation, b=
ut now: =E2=80=9C</span><em>There are cameras in the classrooms; every clas=
s is recorded and uploaded to the cloud. Supervisors can listen to the clas=
ses in real time online, without the teachers knowing. It was only after th=
e semester ended that I learned that over 20 supervisors had once audited m=
y online class</em><span>.=E2=80=9D</span></p></li><li style=3D"margin:8px =
0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;=
box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>Start=
ing this semester, AI tools are tracking her student engagement rates: =E2=
=80=9C</span><em>It can recognize surveillance footage and count how many s=
tudents look up when you=E2=80=99re explaining a particular concept</em><sp=
an>.=E2=80=9D The lesson is disassembled into components that can be quanti=
fied: several minutes must be dedicated to a story-based introduction and s=
everal minutes to explaining key concepts.</span></p></li></ul><p style=3D"=
margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><str=
ong>Qiaoqiao and her colleagues complain that most supervisors have STEM ba=
ckgrounds, so they don=E2=80=99t understand the teaching approach of the hu=
manities and social sciences.</strong><span> </span></p><ul style=3D"margin=
-top:0;padding:0"><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(5=
4,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-lef=
t:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>For example, in a course about researc=
h methods in which she discussed how to conduct fieldwork in villages, Qiao=
qiao once received the following comment, =E2=80=9C</span><em>Too high and =
mighty, just talking about her research experience in class</em><span>.=E2=
=80=9D </span></p></li><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:=
rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;paddin=
g-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>Qiaoqiao recalls another case: =
=E2=80=9C</span><em>I mentioned choosing a field site in class and conclude=
d with a reflection, =E2=80=98Sometimes where you choose to do your fieldwo=
rk is actually an arrangement of fate.=E2=80=99 The supervisor seized on th=
is and wrote in their comments that I was promoting fatalism with students =
and that there were problems with my ideology. I was so angry I laughed.=E2=
=80=9D</em></p></li><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb=
(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-l=
eft:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>From the article: =E2=80=9C</span><e=
m>In her view, university education should be the transmission of =E2=80=98=
Technique and Tao=E2=80=99=E2=80=94the former being methods and skills, the=
 latter the wisdom to understand society and care for oneself and others. H=
owever, these elements, which cannot be simplified into key knowledge point=
s, have lost their place in the new evaluation system</em><span>.=E2=80=9D =
Qiaoqiao muses, =E2=80=9C</span><em>Perhaps it=E2=80=99s because universiti=
es have become increasingly strict in controlling the content and ideas of =
teaching, so they don=E2=80=99t want teachers to have too much room for per=
sonal expression</em><span>.=E2=80=9D</span></p></li></ul><p style=3D"margi=
n:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-size:16px"><strong>A=
longside the rise of AI, changes in the overall economic landscape have cha=
llenged many university humanities and social science programs.</strong></p=
><ul style=3D"margin-top:0;padding:0"><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p =
style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:bo=
rder-box;padding-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>Background context=
 from the article: =E2=80=9C</span><em>Since this summer, at least five uni=
versities have suspended undergraduate admissions for sociology programs, a=
nd many more arts and humanities programs face the risk of closure</em><spa=
n>.=E2=80=9D</span></p></li><li style=3D"margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"c=
olor:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margin-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;p=
adding-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0"><span>Qiaoqiao=E2=80=99s sociology=
 program is still safe for now, but one of her primary tasks is =E2=80=9Cst=
rictly controlling the rate of students changing majors.=E2=80=9D She says,=
 =E2=80=9C</span><em>The transfer rate of a class will affect the disciplin=
e evaluation and even determine whether the program can continue to exist. =
If our major is canceled, our jobs might be in jeopardy, or we might be rea=
ssigned to other colleges like Marxism or Public Administration, where surv=
ival would be even more difficult</em><span>.=E2=80=9D</span></p></li></ul>=
<p style=3D"margin:0 0 20px 0;color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;font-siz=
e:16px"><strong>Even as she realizes that the expansion of humanities enrol=
lment may need to adjust based on market conditions, Qiaoqiao wants her stu=
dents to have the space to read good books, find interesting questions, and=
 seek meaning =E2=80=94 to gain a =E2=80=9Cusefulness of uselessness=E2=80=
=9D mindset.</strong></p><ul style=3D"margin-top:0;padding:0"><li style=3D"=
margin:8px 0 0 32px"><p style=3D"color:rgb(54,55,55);line-height:26px;margi=
n-bottom:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding-left:4px;font-size:16px;margin:0">=
<span>She relays, =E2=80=9C</span><em>Sometimes I feel that universities do=
n=E2=80=99t need to admit so many people to the humanities, nor do they nee=
d so many full-time researchers. There=E2=80=99s no need for everyone to cr=
owd into one track, producing things that are meaningless to the world. The=
 humanities need a freer space to cultivate true masters. But the current e=
